---
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import { github } from '../lib/links';
---

<Page
  title="Privacy"
  description="How Scalar treats the email, calendars, coursework and files it connects to."
  path="/privacy"
  eyebrow="Privacy"
  lede="Scalar reads some of the most sensitive data a person has. These are the principles the software is built around. This is a statement of design intent, not yet a legal privacy policy; one will be published before any hosted service exists."
>
  <h2>Principles</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Collect only what a feature needs. Scopes are requested per feature, not up front.</li>
    <li>Store only what is required to be useful, and say plainly how long it is kept.</li>
    <li>
      Minimise model context. When AI features are used, the model receives the smallest slice of
      data needed for the request, never a dump of your account.
    </li>
    <li>No training on private content by default.</li>
    <li>
      Every integration can be disconnected. Disconnecting revokes access at the provider, deletes
      stored credentials, stops syncing, and asks whether imported data should be kept or removed.
    </li>
    <li>
      You can delete your account, and you will be able to export your data in open formats (JSON,
      CSV, ICS).
    </li>
    <li>External content is data, not instructions. An email cannot tell Scalar what to do.</li>
    <li>
      Telemetry is restrained, never contains private content, and can be fully disabled in
      self-hosted builds.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Credentials</h2>
  <p>
    OAuth refresh tokens are encrypted at rest and only ever handled by a dedicated credential
    service inside the API. Tokens are never logged and never sent to clients.
  </p>

  <h2>Self-hosting</h2>
  <p>
    Because Scalar is open source and self-hostable, you can run the whole system on infrastructure
    you control. Self-hosted builds are the same code as any hosted service; nothing is held back.
  </p>

  <h2>Questions</h2>
  <p>
    Privacy design is discussed in the open. Raise questions in
    <a href={github.discussions}>Discussions</a>, and report vulnerabilities through the
    <a href={github.security}>security policy</a>.
  </p>
</Page>
